    <section class="section bonus" id="bonuses" data-reveal>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="eyebrow">The Editor’s Toolkit</div>
        <h2 class="display">Resources you’ll keep using<br />after the course ends.</h2>
        <div class="bonus-grid" data-stagger>
          <article class="bonus-card"><div class="bonus-icon">✓</div><div><h3>C.H.E.S.S. Editing Checklist</h3><p>A printable, repeatable workflow to keep beside Lightroom until the process becomes second nature.</p></div></article>
          <article class="bonus-card"><div class="bonus-icon">RAW</div><div><h3>RAW Practice Library</h3><p>Work through increasingly difficult files and compare your choices against completed reference edits.</p></div></article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section community" data-reveal>
      <div class="wrap community-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="eyebrow">Continue improving after the course</div>
          <h2 class="display">A community built around better feedback.</h2>
          <p>Submit your edits, learn from selected critiques, and practice articulating why an image works-not just whether you like it.</p>
          <ul class="tick-list">
            <li>Recurring live editing walkthroughs & group critique sessions</li>
            <li>Structured peer feedback using C.H.E.S.S.</li>
            <li>Editing challenges and practice prompts</li>
            <li>A growing archive of real-world critiques</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="community-photo">
          <img src="/assets/community.jpg" alt="Community of photographers learning together" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section about-ivan" data-reveal>
      <div class="wrap about-ivan-grid">
        <figure class="about-ivan-photo">
          <img src="/assets/Ivan.png" alt="Portrait of Ivan Chan" />
        </figure>
        <div class="about-ivan-copy">
          <div class="eyebrow">Meet your instructor</div>
          <div class="about-ivan-heading">
            <figure class="about-ivan-mobile-photo" aria-hidden="true">
              <img src="/assets/Ivan.png" alt="" />
            </figure>
            <h2 class="display">Hi, I’m <span class="marker">Ivan</span>.</h2>
          </div>
          <p>This course exists because of one frustration.</p>
          <p>When you're new to Lightroom, learning is easy. There are thousands of tutorials showing you what every slider does.</p>
          <p>But once you become an <span class="marker">intermediate editor</span>, everything changes.</p>
          <p>The next level isn't hidden behind one secret technique—it’s hidden behind hundreds of small ideas scattered across YouTube videos, books, blogs, paid courses, and years of experimentation.</p>
          <p><strong>I know because I spent years searching for them.</strong></p>
          <p>The funny thing is, those ideas aren't random. They all start to make sense once you understand the <span class="marker">underlying visual principles</span> that connect them. But I struggled to find a resource that tied everything together, leaving me to connect the dots on my own.</p>
          <p>This course is my attempt to do exactly that—to <span class="marker">connect those dots</span> at a fundamental level, so you can understand not just <em>how</em> to edit, but <em>why</em> an edit works.</p>
          <p>It's the collection of everything I wish I'd had when I was trying to go from a good editor to a great one.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="price" id="enroll" data-reveal="scale">
      <div class="wrap price-card">
        <div class="price-copy">
          <div class="eyebrow">Beta access</div>
          <h2 class="display">Help shape the masterclass before launch.</h2>
          <p>Apply to beta test the course for free, work through the material early, and share feedback that helps make the final experience stronger.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="price-box">
          <p>Selected beta testers get free course access in exchange for thoughtful feedback on the lessons, exercises, and overall learning experience.</p>
          <a class="btn" style="background:var(--lime);color:#111;margin-top:18px" href="https://forms.gle/NXVrTfgdjUeVwoFJ7" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apply for free beta access ↗</a>
          <p class="small">Applications open through Google Forms. I’ll follow up with selected testers directly.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section faq" data-reveal>
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="eyebrow">Questions</div>
        <h2 class="display">Before you join.</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details open><summary>Who is this course for?</summary><p>Intermediate photographers who already understand the main Lightroom tools but know their edits could be better, they just don't know how. The deep understanding of visual principles you'll walk away with from this course as well as the ability to pick and use the right tools will eliminate the whole guessing game, regardless of what style you want to create.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Can I take the course as a beginner?</summary><p>The course is more geared towards intermediate editors, so it does not cover how each tool in Lightroom works in detail, but if you are willing to supplement the course material with the plethora of free Lightroom Youtube tutorials explaining the tools in detail, you should be fine. It'll probably just take you longer to complete the course compared to an intermediate-level editor.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Is this course designed for photos of any subject matter?</summary><p>The course was created with landscape/travel/adventure photographers in mind, so there is a bunch of material that talks about shooting in nature, how natural light affects colors, contrast, etc... and how to work with it. This may be less useful for photographers that shoot indoors. However, the vast majority of the content of this course is applicable to editing photos of any subject matter</p></details>
          <details><summary>Will this teach me to replicate your exact style?</summary><p>You will see how I edit my images through full walkthroughs, so you will see the thinking and techniques behind my edits, but the goal is not to teach you to clone a single look. The principles are designed to help you build whatever style feels true to your work, whether that's a style similar to mine or not.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Do I need Lightroom Classic?</summary><p>The exercises as presented in a Lightroom catalog designed for Lightroom Classic. If you don't have access to Lightroom Classic, you can still do the exercises in Lightroom, however, you will not have the benefit of seeing snapshots that I've created to show you the finished exercises for you to compare your results to. If you have the choice to use Lightroom Classic, I would recommend it. </p></details>
          <details><summary>Will I receive feedback on my own images?</summary><p>The paid community includes selected live critiques, structured critique threads, peer feedback, and personalized review. Its a first-come-first-served environment so as long as you engage quickly, your chances of getting personal reviews will be high. Regardless, you will be guaranteed to benefit from seeing all the personalized reviews of other photographers' work.</p></details>
          <details><summary>Why should I pick this course over others?</summary>
          <h3 class="faq-subtitle">Principles-first approach</h3>
          <p>Most courses focus heavily on tools and techniques, and less time on the underlying visual principles and understanding that determines what techniques should be used. In this course, my goal is to lead with the principles, so that you become an editor that can make clear intentional decisions rather than just throwing a bag of tricks at the wall and seeing what sticks. I will also share techniques, but these are of secondary importance to the principles. Because once you learn to see properly, you’ll naturally know what you need to do, and it will unlock your own creativity to achieve the vision. </p>
          <h3 class="faq-subtitle">Practice makes perfect</h3>
          <p>Secondly, most courses lack exercises and even if they do have them, it’s little more than a suggestion to find a photo in your own catalog to practice a technique on. Sometimes, you don’t have a photo that fits the bill or it may take a lot of effort to find one. In this course, I give you very specific exercises designed to lock in the learnings from specific lessons, isolating stages in the edit so you can see the effect of a singular technique or theory application without having to worry about other edits. All Raws are provided. I’ve picked up a lot of skills in my life, and I know from experience that knowledge does not become solidified until you put it into action. And the most useful time to put that knowledge into action is as soon as you’ve learned it. </p></details>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

